  "ai_language_tip": "Use Hebrew (he) by default; Arabic for businesses in Arab towns and many East Jerusalem/Galilee shops; English is widely understood and acceptable as a fallback; Russian is common among older staff in some cities.",
  "english_ok": "often — Most younger Israelis and tourist-facing businesses speak good English; less at small shops, older staff and government offices.",
  "politeness": "Informal and direct; 'Shalom' and get to the point; first names are normal; say you call on behalf of someone; 'toda' to close — overt formality is not expected.",
  "name_tip": "Bookings go under family name or first name; Hebrew systems store names in Hebrew letters, so spell the surname; clinics also ask the ID number (owner only).",
  "phone_tip": "Mobile 10 digits starting 05 (050-123-4567 style, +972 drops the 0); landlines 02/03/04/08/09 plus 7 digits; star short codes (*1234 style) for businesses; SMS confirmations standard.",
  "time_tip": "24-hour clock in writing; spoken 'shmone va-hetsi' = 8:30, 'reva le-shmone' = 7:45, 'sheva va-reva' = 7:15; context (ba-erev/ba-boker) decides AM/PM — read back as 24-hour.",
  "number_tip": "Hebrew numbers have gender (masculine/feminine forms); phone numbers read in groups (050-123-4567); prices in shekels; Western digits used everywhere.",
  "avoid_calling": "Avoid Friday afternoon through Saturday night (Shabbat) for most Jewish-owned businesses, Jewish holidays (Rosh Hashanah, Yom Kippur — nearly everything shuts), and calls after 21:00; for Arab-owned businesses avoid Friday noon prayer and Ramadan iftar.",
  "closed_days": "Working week Sunday–Thursday, Friday half day; Shabbat (Friday sunset to Saturday nightfall) closes most shops, offices and public transport, though many Tel Aviv restaurants open; Jewish holidays close offices; Muslim and Christian businesses may close Friday or Sunday instead.",
  "booking_channels": "Restaurants via Tabit and Ontopo; delivery via Wolt and 10bis; health via the Kupat Holim apps (Clalit, Maccabi, Meuhedet, Leumit) and call centres; taxis via Gett and Yango; government via gov.il online queues; payments by card and Bit.",
  "voice_menu": "Health funds, banks, telecoms, Israel Post and government use Hebrew IVR with Arabic/Russian/English options; restaurants and small businesses answer in person or send booking links.",
  "callback_habits": "Booking platforms send SMS confirmations with a link to change or cancel; clinics send SMS reminders; small businesses use WhatsApp heavily.",
